Latest News
SARA EISEN: Let’s get the market outlook for 2024 with one of the biggest names in banking. Our next guest sees three rate cuts on deck for next year from the Fed, says it’s likely we’ll see some pain for the economy. That doesn’t mean those stocks will struggle. Joining us now is Charles Schwab’s CEO, Walt Bettinger. Walt, good to see you. Welcome.
WALT BETTINGER: Well, thanks, Sara. I appreciate the invitation to be on your program.
SARA: Yeah. No, it’s always good to hear from you. What are you seeing from retail investors right now with this powerful rally that’s going on now eight weeks?
WALT: It has been a strong rally, but retail investors are still very cautious. Currently, we’re seeing a little bit over 50% of them have a bearish view. Now, that’s improved quite a bit from a year ago, when it was about three-fourths of them with a bearish view on the market. But they’re still fairly cautious right now. A lot of concerns about the potential for inflation not falling as rapidly as maybe some are projecting, and also a lot of concerns about the political environment that we’re operating in, and what 2024 could bring.
SARA: So do you see that as a tailwind for the markets, that people are still feeling cautious, and they could be about to deploy a lot more cash?
WALT: Gosh, it’s such a tough call in the short run as to whether consumer and investor sentiment is going to drive the market. I do think probably the actions of the Fed are likely to have more of an impact maybe than whether sentiment is 50% negative or maybe turns a little bit more positive.
SARA: I mean, what a year it’s been, Walt, for Schwab, for regional banks, for the entire banking system. And I know you’re in quiet period, so can’t talk necessarily on performance, but just curious what lessons you have learned from this year about managing the business in this rising rate cycle.
WALT: Well, one thing we know is that individual stock performance can be driven by narratives on a short-term basis. Sometimes those are positive narratives, sometimes they’re negative narratives. But at Schwab, we’ve always tried to take a very long-term perspective in the managing of the business, and that means a focus on clients, a focus on client metrics. If you actually take a step back and look at 2023, it was a pretty strong year for our firm overall. Clients entrusted us with over $300 billion in net new assets, still a rather astonishing number. Our pre-tax margins just adjusted for the one-time costs around the integration state in the 40% range. It was a strong year. And, of course, oh, by the way, we did all that while completing about 90% of the largest, most complex integration in the history of our industry as we combined with Ameritrade. So sure, in the short run there were some challenges, driven in part by reality and in part by a narrative but in the long run, we’re right on track for the long-term growth that we feel really bullish about for Schwab and for our clients and our stockholders.
